<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomism is the philosophical and theological school that arose from the work and thought of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> (1225–1274), a Dominican philosopher, theologian and Doctor of the Church. Aquinas drew on Greek, Roman, Islamic and Jewish philosophers, largely followed Aristotelian terminology and metaphysics, and held that the world can be known as it is; his Summa Theologica remains a central point of reference for the philosophy and theology of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-xiii">Pope Leo XIII</a>'s encyclical Aeterni Patris of 1879 spurred a revival known as Neothomism, which held sway as the dominant philosophy of the Roman Catholic Church until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-vatican-council">Second Vatican Council</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Aquinas held that truth is to be accepted wherever it is found, and he was a realist, believing that the world can be known as it is. He wrote commentaries on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, whom he called simply the Philosopher. A being, in Thomist philosophy, is an essence that exists: its quiddity, or whatness, together with its esse, or existence. He defined the good as what all things strive for, so that evil is a lack of good.</p><p>Aquinas held that the existence of God can be demonstrated by reason, and the Summa Theologica gives five ways of doing so: from motion, from efficient cause, from contingency, from degree and from the end of things. God is the sole being whose existence is the same as His essence, for which Aquinas used the term actus purus. Thomists define the soul as the substantial form of living beings, and human beings alone have rational and immortal souls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first period of Thomism runs from Aquinas's teaching, beginning at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1256, until his canonisation in 1325. In 1277 Étienne Tempier, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Paris, condemned 219 propositions, twenty of them Thomistic; the condemnation was withdrawn after Aquinas was canonised. Thomism declined in the modern period that Descartes opened with his Discourse on the Method in 1637. Salvatore Roselli published a six-volume Summa philosophica in 1777 and is considered to have laid the foundation of Neothomism.</p><p>The revival of the mid-19th century drew on Tommaso Maria Zigliara, Josef Kleutgen and Gaetano Sanseverino, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-xiii">Pope Leo XIII</a>'s Aeterni Patris of 1879 gave it impetus. In 1914 Pope Pius X's decree Postquam sanctissimus presented 24 theses as containing the principles of Aquinas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Aquinas shifted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scholasticism">Scholasticism</a> away from neoplatonism and towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, and the school that followed is counted among the most influential in philosophy. Several schools of interpretation arose in the 20th century. Existential Thomism, whose chief proponent was Étienne Gilson, stresses Aquinas's doctrine of being as existence, and Jacques Maritain sought to harmonise Thomism with personalism and democracy. Transcendental Thomism, associated with Joseph Maréchal, Karl Rahner and Bernard Lonergan, seeks to reconcile Aquinas with Kant. Lublin Thomism, whose best-known proponent was Karol Wojtyla, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a>, draws on phenomenology.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thomism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
